-- title:
--   Γ(4) is a free group
-- statement:
--   The assertion concerns the principal congruence subgroup of level $4$ in $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$, namely the subgroup `CongruenceSubgroup.Gamma 4` of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ consisting of those integral matrices of determinant $1$ whose reduction modulo $4$ is the identity matrix. The statement is that the group formed by the elements of this subgroup, with the induced multiplication, is a free group in the sense of Mathlib's `IsFreeGroup`: there exists a subset of $\Gamma(4)$ on which the group is free, equivalently $\Gamma(4)$ is isomorphic as a group to the free group on some type. No rank is asserted, and no further data or hypotheses enter: the theorem takes no arguments and has no side conditions, the level $4$ being fixed numerically.
--
--   That $\Gamma(4)$ is free is the classical consequence of Sanov's theorem, which exhibits the group generated by $\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\0&1\end{pmatrix}$ and $\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\2&1\end{pmatrix}$ as free of rank two and containing $\Gamma(4)$, combined with the Nielsen–Schreier theorem on subgroups of free groups. It is used in the construction of perfect pairings on parabolic cohomology of modular curves and in the level-raising arguments that depend on those pairings.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_PDPairing_isFreeGroup_Gamma_four.lean

